---
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import ContentLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/ContentLayout.astro";
import { withBase } from "astro-theme-university/url";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/hero-street-queue.avif";
import assessmentIcon from "../assets/icons/assessment.svg";
import lecturesIcon from "../assets/icons/lectures.svg";
import peopleIcon from "../assets/icons/people.svg";
import sessionsIcon from "../assets/icons/sessions.svg";
import timetableIcon from "../assets/icons/timetable.svg";
import CourseStats from "../components/CourseStats.astro";
import LittlesLaw from "../components/LittlesLaw.astro";
import Photo from "../components/Photo.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { navGroups, sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import NavGroups from "../components/NavGroups.astro";
---

<ContentLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code} ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="A street in gold and black, a queue of people with backpacks waiting behind a rope barrier along a shopfront under trees, with the road and pavement empty away to the left"
  {...siteConfig}
>
  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="lede">
    Every queue you have ever stood in was designed by somebody, and almost none
    of them bothered to tell you how it was done. That is more or less the whole
    problem, and it is what we spend the semester on. In week 1 you will pick a
    queue of your own, and you will keep that same one all the way through to
    week 12.
  </p>

  <CourseStats />

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    In week 1 you will pick a queue to work on. It can be almost anything you
    like, whether that is a till, a counter, a platform or a help desk, as long
    as it is somewhere you can realistically stand next to twice a week without
    it becoming a chore. Do take a bit of care over the choice, because whatever
    you settle on is yours for the rest of the semester and swapping later is
    more painful than it sounds.
  </p>

  <Photo
    name="shopfront-queue"
    caption="Somebody chose how this works, even if they would not put it that way themselves. They chose how many people serve at once, in what order, and what happens to the person who turns up at the wrong moment. It looks like nothing much from the outside, but by about week 5 you will be able to read most of it straight off the pavement."
  />

  <p>
    For the first fortnight you will be measuring the queue and its times by
    hand, with a pen, and we fully expect everyone to measure it badly to begin
    with. Please do not worry about that. Everybody gets better at it quite
    quickly, and the early mess is genuinely part of the process rather than a
    sign that you have picked the wrong site. By around week 4 most people can
    work out their queue's arrival rate, the rough shape of its service times,
    and how much of the wait is really the tax the queue pays for being
    unpredictable rather than simply busy. Alongside all of that you will keep a
    record of every time somebody gets served out of order, and of what that
    cost the people standing behind them. The last step is the one the marking
    cares about most. You write down what it would take to change the queue, you
    predict the effect before anybody tells you the answer, and then you name
    whoever ends up waiting longer once your change is in. Somebody always does,
    and saying so plainly is most of what we are looking for.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then in week 12 you defend that change in front of the room. If you manage to
    persuade the room, and a fair few of you do, the obvious next step is to take
    it to whoever actually runs the queue.
  </p>

  <LittlesLaw />

  <h2>What the course thinks</h2>
  <p>
    Honestly, we think the arithmetic is the easy half. There are only four
    results in the whole course, and between them they are more than enough to
    embarrass most of the people who design queues for a living. Little's Law
    will tell you how long a line has been waiting without you timing a single
    person in it, which still feels like a trick the first time you do it.
    Kingman will tell you why a counter got noticeably worse in the month after
    somebody made it more efficient, which is the kind of thing that is very
    satisfying to be able to explain.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="pull">
    <p>
      Spare capacity is not waste. It is what you are bought instead of a
      queue.
    </p>
    <footer>Week 3, variability is the tax</footer>
  </blockquote>

  <p>
    The hard half is the question that none of those equations will answer for
    you, and it is the one most people find genuinely uncomfortable. A queue
    discipline is a ranking of people, and nearly every one you will come across
    was set without anybody quite admitting that is what it is. Emergency triage
    is the honourable exception here, because it ranks people openly and says
    why, and that is exactly why we give a whole week to it. The week after that
    goes to the opposite case, which is the queues that publish nothing at
    all.
  </p>

  <h2>What students did with it</h2>
  <div class="voices">
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        I came in wanting to prove the bus interchange was badly run. It is not.
        It is running at ninety-four percent and nobody has told the people who
        set the timetable what that costs.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, environments and design</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        The redesign studio in week 11 is where my proposal fell apart. Three
        people found the group my change would have made worse inside four
        minutes, which is far better than finding it in the marking.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, public policy</footer>
    </blockquote>
    <blockquote>
      <p>
        I measured the main entrance at the gardens where I work weekends. The
        change I proposed in week 11 is the one they trialled in October, and
        the queue at opening is about four minutes shorter.
      </p>
      <footer>Third year, environments and design</footer>
    </blockquote>
  </div>

  <h2>What you can do by week 12</h2>
  <p>
    There are six of them in the end, and between them they are the reason the
    weeks run in the order they do rather than in some other order.
  </p>
  <ul class="outcomes">
    <li>
      <strong>Put a number on a wait you never timed.</strong> It really is just
      two counts and one division, and once you have the knack you can do it
      from thirty metres away on more or less any queue at all.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Say why a place got worse after it got busier.</strong> Waiting
      does not rise neatly in step with demand, it accelerates, and you will be
      able to show somebody where the cliff is using their own numbers rather
      than an argument.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Choose between one line and many, and defend it.</strong> Pooling
      wins on the arithmetic every single time, and yet there are five perfectly
      real reasons why your supermarket still does not do it.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Name who a queue is charging.</strong> Every order of service is a
      ranking of people whether anybody says so or not, and you will be able to
      work out what that ranking costs the person at the back, in minutes.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Measure a queue that publishes nothing.</strong> Waiting lists,
      backlogs and referral pathways all behave like queues underneath, so with
      a bit of work you can estimate the length of a line that nobody is willing
      to tell you the length of.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Change one and predict the result in advance.</strong> You write
      the prediction down, along with the arithmetic that produced it, before
      anybody tells you whether you are right. That last part is harder than it
      sounds and it is the whole point.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p class="lede-sm">
    If you are ever going to have a say in how people wait, then this is
    probably the course for you.
  </p>
  <p>
    Quite a lot of the people who wait are waiting in a place that somebody drew
    on purpose. That might be a park with one gate and four paths leading to it,
    or a ride with a switchback built to hold ninety minutes of people, or a
    festival laid out with six bars at one end and the toilet block at the
    other. Station concourses, stadium exits and museum entrances on a wet
    Sunday all belong on the same list. The point is that if you are going to
    design public space at all, then you are going to design a queue as well,
    and the drawing tends to settle it whether or not anybody in the room ever
    says the word out loud.
  </p>
  <p>
    Plenty of others are waiting at a counter of some kind, whether that is a
    till, a help desk, a pharmacy window, passport control, triage in an
    emergency department, or a phone line that only answers between nine and
    four. Others again are waiting in a list they cannot see at all, such as a
    housing register, a visa subclass, a specialist referral or an insurance
    claim. Those are easily the hardest queues in the course, mostly because
    nothing about them is on display and you have to work out how to measure
    them anyway. And some of what waits is not a person at all, which is why we
    get a good number of students who write schedulers, rate limiters and
    request queues.
  </p>
  <p>
    The course is open to third-year students in any degree at all. You need to
    be reasonably comfortable with algebra, averages and a spreadsheet, and that
    really is the whole prerequisite, since there is no calculus anywhere in the
    course and no proof anywhere in the assessment. What you do need, and we
    would rather say this up front, is a willingness to stand outside a shop for
    an hour with a clipboard, in the cold, while people look at you and wonder
    what you are doing.
  </p>

  <h2>What it is not</h2>
  <p>
    It is worth being clear that this is not a queueing theory course. A proper
    queueing theory course would spend the whole semester on Markov chains and
    never once send you outside. Only four results appear here, and each of them
    earns its place because you genuinely need it to say something true about a
    real line. There is also no exam, in case you were wondering.
  </p>

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card
      title="Timetable"
      href="/timetable/"
      image={timetableIcon}
      imageAlt="A grid of twelve weeks, one of them outlined"
    >
      <p>
        The whole semester sits on one page, with the two busy stretches
        marked so you can plan around them.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Lectures"
      href="/lectures/"
      image={lecturesIcon}
      imageAlt="A board with three lines of writing, faced by a row of people"
    >
      <p>
        Twelve weeks, and each one makes a claim you could reasonably argue
        with.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title={sessionLabels.plural}
      href="/sessions/"
      image={sessionsIcon}
      imageAlt="A clipboard with two struck tally groups and a third in progress"
    >
      <p>
        Two hours a week, spent mostly outdoors and usually with a stopwatch in
        your hand.
      </p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="Assessment"
      href="/assessments/"
      image={assessmentIcon}
      imageAlt="Four bars of different heights standing on a rule"
    >
      <p>Four pieces, all of them about the same queue.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card
      title="People"
      href="/people/"
      image={peopleIcon}
      imageAlt="Four figures side by side, the last one drawn as an outline"
    >
      <p>Who teaches it, which weeks, and when they are free.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>

  <p class="footnote">
    Needing more time is ordinary and asking early is free.
    <a href={withBase("/extensions/")}>Ask for an extension</a>, or read the
    <a href={withBase("/policies/")}>policies</a> before you need them.
  </p>

  <NavGroups groups={navGroups} />
</ContentLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* The opening paragraph carries the whole argument, so it gets the weight
     the headings would otherwise take. One size step, no second colour. */
  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    margin-block-end: 2.5rem;
  }

  .lede-sm {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    margin-block: 0 1rem;
  }

  .outcomes {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.1rem;
  }

  .outcomes li {
    margin-block-end: 0.7rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
  }

  .pull {
    margin-block: 1.75rem;
    padding-inline-start: 1.25rem;
    border-inline-start: 2px solid var(--at-primary);
  }

  .pull p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .pull footer,
  .voices footer {
    margin-block-start: 0.5rem;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  .voices {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
    gap: 1rem;
    margin-block: 1.5rem 2rem;
  }

  .voices blockquote {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 1rem 1.1rem;
    border: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
  }

  .voices blockquote p {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
  }

  .footnote {
    margin-block-start: 2rem;
    padding-block-start: 1.25rem;
    border-block-start: 1px solid var(--at-border);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
  }

  @media (width < 60rem) {
    .voices {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }

  @media (width < 44rem) {
    .lede {
      font-size: 1.05rem;
    }

    .pull p {
      font-size: 1.1rem;
    }
  }
</style>
